00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:46.373
MARK MANDEL: So we've got
a bunch of announcements

00:02:46.373 --> 00:02:48.580
around, strangely enough,
containers and Kubernetes.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.620
I think one of the ones
I'm really excited about

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:55.510
is a product called gVisor which
we open sourced back in May 2.

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:56.780
This is really interesting.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:00.399
This is a new kind of sandbox
that provides secure isolation

00:03:00.399 --> 00:03:02.440
for containers while still
being more lightweight

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:03.350
than a virtual machine.

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:05.230
So it integrates with
Docker and Kubernetes,

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:07.870
but really, I think what's
kind of exciting about this

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:11.200
is it really imposes a very
strong, secure isolation

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.790
layer for when you want to run
code that you don't necessarily

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:14.290
trust.

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:16.810
Maybe it comes from outside
or you're opening things up,

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:19.000
or maybe you just want the
extra layer of security.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.340
I think it's really, really
cool in that it really

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.410
allows you to be
able to run things

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:24.680
in a very secure environment.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:25.763
Now, there is a trade-off.

00:03:25.763 --> 00:03:28.810
At its core there is
a userspace kernel

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:30.970
that basically emulates
the Linux kernel, which

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:34.150
does mean that the
flexibility that you get

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:36.607
comes at the price of higher
per-system call overhead.

00:03:36.607 --> 00:03:38.440
And there are some
details in the blog post.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:39.970
But this is really exciting.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:41.387
Look for integrations
with Docker.

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:43.803
There's a sample in the blog
post about how you can run it

00:03:43.803 --> 00:03:45.820
with Docker right now,
some experimental support

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.530
potentially within
Kubernetes as well.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:48.890
Its going to come down the line.

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:50.530
So there's some really
interesting opportunities

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.420
here for being able
to build some really

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:54.317
interesting platforms
for customers

00:03:54.317 --> 00:03:55.400
on top of this technology.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:56.440
I'm really excited about it.

