00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:37.766
It's fun.

00:06:37.766 --> 00:06:39.390
People pay me to
write open source code

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:42.190
and fly around the world,
so my life is pretty cool.

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:44.530
MELANIE WARRICK: And how did
you get involved with Spark

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:46.260
and Beam to begin with?

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:49.740
HOLDEN KARAU: So I got involved
with Spark from the last time

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:51.360
I was working at Google.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:53.130
There was this thing
called FlumeJava,

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:54.601
which was kind of cool.

00:06:54.601 --> 00:06:56.850
But I couldn't use it for
any of my personal projects.

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:59.010
And somehow back then, I
still had time in my life

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:00.630
for personal projects.

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:05.080
And I was like, I really want to
use this software but I can't.

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:08.610
So I looked around for an
open source reimplementation

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:11.920
of Google technology, which
is a pretty common approach

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:13.950
in the big data world,
and I found Spark.

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:15.440
And I was like, oh, cool.

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:16.860
This uses functional
programming.

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:18.650
This is awesome.

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:21.150
That's sort of how I
got started with Spark.

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:22.720
And that was a lot of fun.

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:23.550
MELANIE WARRICK:
Was that in Scala,

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:24.450
when you were working with it?

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:25.830
HOLDEN KARAU: I mean,
Spark started in Scala.

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:27.930
And it's still in Scala
now, but you can use it

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:30.370
in other languages as well.

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:32.650
But for me, Scala was
actually a pretty good fit.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:34.950
I'd just come from
Foursquare where

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:36.960
everything was built in Scala.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:39.750
And I really like
functional programming.

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:42.450
I think it's kind of a nice way
of thinking about the world.

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:44.670
And so I really
enjoyed a framework

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:48.490
that really put a functional
approach to dealing with data.

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:50.580
I felt it was a really cool fit.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:51.400
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:52.608
Let's take a small step back.

00:07:52.608 --> 00:07:54.600
For those people who
aren't as familiar

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:57.390
with, say, Spark or Beam,
what's the kind of problem

00:07:57.390 --> 00:07:59.190
that those products
are trying to solve?

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:01.080
HOLDEN KARAU: Both
Spark and Beam,

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.150
and a lot of other
things in this space,

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:04.740
essentially solve
the problem of--

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:07.700
you have a lot of data and you
want to do something with it,

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:12.330
and it's too much data to fit on
a single computer or maybe even

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:13.410
a few single computers.

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:16.350
And it's about splitting the
problems up, distributing

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:18.630
both the data and
the computation,

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:20.700
and then collecting the
results back in a way

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:21.970
that you can use them.

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:24.922
So some of the classic
problems where people use this

00:08:24.922 --> 00:08:26.880
are things like training
recommendation systems

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:28.500
where you have a
lot of information

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:30.507
from all of your
different users.

00:08:30.507 --> 00:08:32.340
Other times it's things
like fraud detection

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:34.508
where we'll have a
lot of transactions

00:08:34.508 --> 00:08:35.799
and we'll want to train models.

00:08:35.799 --> 00:08:37.539
It's not just machine learning.

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:39.470
It's also often used for ETL.

00:08:39.470 --> 00:08:42.659
We might have an old legacy
database which we also

00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:44.670
want to expose in a nicer way.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:48.090
And so you might have a batch
job which comes in and picks up

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:49.590
your data from your
legacy database

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:53.614
and makes it available to people
to do exploratory analytics on.

00:08:53.614 --> 00:08:55.530
MELANIE WARRICK: And
where did Beam come from?

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:57.690
HOLDEN KARAU: Beam came
from a large number

00:08:57.690 --> 00:08:59.130
of different Google projects.

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:03.690
It's sort of the grandchild,
in a way, of MillWheel

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:05.130
and other things like this.

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:08.730
So it's the grandchild of a
lot of Google big data tools.

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:10.800
It brings a lot of the
learnings that we've

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:13.770
had from working on MapReduce
and working on things

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:16.440
like MillWheel and
other analytics tools,

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:19.450
and brings them all
together into one place.

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:22.860
The idea is that Beam will
provide a portability layer

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:26.280
from Google Dataflow, which
exposes all of these cool tools

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:29.700
to people to use, to things
besides Google Dataflow.

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:33.480
Because as much as we love
people that use Google tools,

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:35.700
not everyone wants
to write their code

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:37.140
against just a Google backend.

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:39.760
They want to be able to
run it in multiple places.

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:42.810
Some people have
analytics jobs which

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:45.870
they need to do both on data
which is less secretive,

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:47.700
and then they have
similar jobs which

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:50.250
have to be run on
super-secret data which

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:53.400
they can't put in the cloud yet
for, maybe, regulatory reasons.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:56.400
And so having a system which is
able to both run in the cloud

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:58.440
and run on-prem is
a really cool idea.

00:09:58.440 --> 00:09:59.940
And it's one where
we have, perhaps,

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:03.210
not quite delivered what
we set out to do initially.

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:05.220
But I'm really excited
about the direction

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:06.630
we're taking with it this year.

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:09.277
I just yesterday
was at Flink Forward

00:10:09.277 --> 00:10:11.610
where we were talking about
some of the work which we've

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:15.150
been doing around making
Beam on top of Flink, which

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:17.870
is an open source
Apache project, a better

00:10:17.870 --> 00:10:19.230
experience for people.

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:20.660
MARK MANDEL: So
this sounds great.

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:22.479
But you work on
Spark and on Beam.

00:10:22.479 --> 00:10:23.270
HOLDEN KARAU: Yeah.

00:10:23.270 --> 00:10:25.690
MARK MANDEL: Are they
competing open source products?

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:27.470
Or what's the
relationship there?

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:30.140
HOLDEN KARAU: So
it's complicated.

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:31.190
I want to be clear--

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:33.410
there's also Apache Flink
and other open source

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:35.916
runners which do similar
data processing tools.

00:10:35.916 --> 00:10:37.790
And we all take slightly
different approaches

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:39.165
to solving the same problem.

00:10:39.165 --> 00:10:40.790
And at the end of
the day, what happens

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:44.120
is we tend to go and steal the
best ideas from each other.

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:46.380
Because it's open source
and we can do that.

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:47.630
Everyone wins this way, right?

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:50.240
And we also, by competing with
each other to some degree,

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:52.470
push the state of
the market forward.

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:55.682
And so in some places, Beam
and Spark are competitors.

00:10:55.682 --> 00:10:57.140
In other places,
you could actually

00:10:57.140 --> 00:10:59.300
use Beam on top of Spark.

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:00.980
And so they can
be complementary.

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:04.970
And it's really confusing if
you're not from an open source

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:07.910
background to think of
this mixture of competing

00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:09.770
and cooperating
at the same time.

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:11.325
But I think it's a
really good model.

00:11:11.325 --> 00:11:12.950
MARK MANDEL: Are
there particular cases

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:16.670
where you would use, say, one
or the other, or together?

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:21.440
HOLDEN KARAU: The cases where
you would use Beam is probably

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:23.420
more around streaming.

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:25.250
Historically, Spark
Streaming has had,

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:28.040
maybe, not as great
support as in Beam.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:30.505
Although Spark Streaming is,
of course, also improving.

00:11:30.505 --> 00:11:31.880
One of the things
which continues

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:35.270
to happen in these places
is we'll do something cool,

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:36.790
someone else will
do something cool.

00:11:36.790 --> 00:11:38.789
Sometimes it will actually
be the same developer

00:11:38.789 --> 00:11:40.430
in two different projects.

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:42.050
Like there's pieces
of code that I've

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:44.210
written which are
not the same code,

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:47.250
but accomplish the same
task in both Spark and Beam.

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:48.230
Anyways, back to Beam.

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:49.813
You're more likely
to use it if you're

00:11:49.813 --> 00:11:52.640
interested in portability
between Google

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:56.390
Cloud and an on-prem solution,
or other things like that.

00:11:56.390 --> 00:11:59.630
Spark, though, for
example, has, really,

00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:02.180
much more options in
terms of machine learning.

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:05.180
From a programmatic
standpoint, the Beam SDK

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:08.275
is very much focused around
whole program optimization.

00:12:08.275 --> 00:12:09.650
And it's really
cool, and you can

00:12:09.650 --> 00:12:11.525
do a bunch of interesting
things as a result.

00:12:11.525 --> 00:12:13.880
But because of the way how
it was initially built,

00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:16.156
it's kind of difficult to
do iterative algorithms

00:12:16.156 --> 00:12:17.780
like gradient descent,
which are really

00:12:17.780 --> 00:12:18.980
common in machine learning.

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:21.830
And so Spark has a
more full-featured set

00:12:21.830 --> 00:12:25.550
of machine learning tools that
are available for it right now.

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:27.590
Although of course,
Beam is looking

00:12:27.590 --> 00:12:30.290
at what it can do to improve
its machine learning options.

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:33.020
And none of these things
really ever remain static.

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:33.740
MELANIE WARRICK: So
you've talked about how

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:35.100
they're both open source.

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:37.550
We also have them at GCP.

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:38.729
And so how does that work?

00:12:38.729 --> 00:12:40.520
They're managed versions
of these products?

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:40.760
HOLDEN KARAU: Indeed.

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:42.510
MELANIE WARRICK: And
if so, what are they?

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:45.200
HOLDEN KARAU: Glorious employer
provides excellent managed

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:46.106
solutions.

00:12:46.106 --> 00:12:48.230
MELANIE WARRICK: I wasn't
teeing that one up at all

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:49.080
for that one.

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:51.330
HOLDEN KARAU: There's Dataflow
where you can run Beam.

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:53.990
Beam on top of Dataflow is
a really good experience.

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:57.090
Spark actually has two
different managed backends

00:12:57.090 --> 00:12:59.660
which you could use at
Google, and different levels

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:02.430
of support associated with
those different backends,

00:13:02.430 --> 00:13:05.100
both in terms of just
functionality and actual

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:05.600
support.

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:07.790
So Dataproc is probably
what most people

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:11.280
think of as the managed
solution for Spark at Google.

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:13.570
I'm really excited about
where we're going with that.

00:13:13.570 --> 00:13:16.710
There's a lot of exciting
improvements we can make there.

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:18.640
The other one, which is new--

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:21.221
in Spark 2.3 we added
support for Kubernetes.

00:13:21.221 --> 00:13:22.970
And this is really
exciting because it now

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:25.550
means that Spark can run
on top of things like GKE.

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:29.000
And actually, it's a little
clunky to use right now,

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:30.860
but with Spark on
top of Kubernetes

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:33.170
you really need a
separate distributed file

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:34.550
system available.

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:37.020
Because it doesn't
tend to ship with one,

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:38.720
whereas Spark on
Dataproc traditionally

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:41.120
ships with an HDFS
YARN provider.

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:43.816
So running Spark on
Kubernetes in the cloud

00:13:43.816 --> 00:13:45.440
is a much better
experience because you

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:49.030
tend to have an object store,
like GCS, available to use.

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:50.020
So it's pretty cool.

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:52.700
Spark on GKE is like
a managed service,

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:55.010
but if you run
into difficulties--

00:13:55.010 --> 00:13:59.120
I mean, it's like a managed
service on the Kubernetes side.

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:01.580
And if you run into
Spark-specific difficulties,

00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:03.770
you're probably not going
to get as much support

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:05.510
as you would if you
were in Dataproc.

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:08.240
But it's a really fun way to
try out cutting-edge stuff.

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:11.240
And I actually have a blog
post on the GCP big data

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:14.540
blog about how you can try
out different new versions

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:15.052
of Spark.

00:14:15.052 --> 00:14:17.510
And actually-- not that I'm
just trying to get people to do

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:18.650
my work for me--

00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:19.250
MELANIE WARRICK: But you are.

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:21.208
MARK MANDEL: It's called
delegation, I believe.

00:14:21.208 --> 00:14:22.850
HOLDEN KARAU: Well, no, no.

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:25.160
It's delegation when
we're at the same company.

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:27.430
And it's valued
partnership building.

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:28.680
MELANIE WARRICK: There you go.

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:31.320
HOLDEN KARAU: That's going in
my personal accomplishments

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:33.140
if anyone actually
picks up these tasks.

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:34.070
Please do.

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:35.900
So one of the things
which I would really

00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:38.300
like the community's help
with-- and especially if you

00:14:38.300 --> 00:14:39.860
are a Spark user--
if you're not,

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:42.180
don't feel obligated
to do this at all.

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:45.680
But come release time,
we have this process

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:47.760
where we make candidates
for release, right?

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:50.510
And it's like, we think this
is a pretty good release.

00:14:50.510 --> 00:14:52.610
But we're limited
by the workloads

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:54.440
that we know about, right?

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:57.202
And this is open source
"we," not the Google "we."

00:14:57.202 --> 00:14:58.910
And so the people
involved with the Spark

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:01.790
project, the core developers,
it's a large set of people

00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:06.600
but it's not completely
representative of actual users.

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:09.590
And if you wanted to,
and you were using GCP,

00:15:09.590 --> 00:15:13.040
you could try out your workloads
on top of Spark with GKE

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:14.540
with one of these
release candidates

00:15:14.540 --> 00:15:16.670
with the instructions
in that blog post.

00:15:16.670 --> 00:15:18.470
And if it doesn't
work, you can let

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:21.320
us know so we don't
release broken software

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:23.230
and have to patch
it later, right?

00:15:23.230 --> 00:15:26.360
You can help us catch
our bugs earlier.

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:28.230
And we can make better
software together.

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:28.880
MARK MANDEL: So if
you already have,

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:31.120
maybe, a production
workload set up on, say,

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:33.560
GKE or something like that,
you could spin it up and just

00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:35.300
take it for a test run
and see what happens.

00:15:35.300 --> 00:15:35.810
HOLDEN KARAU: Totally.

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:37.580
But definitely
take it for a test

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:40.070
run in an account
with lower permissions

00:15:40.070 --> 00:15:41.790
so that you don't
overwrite your data,

00:15:41.790 --> 00:15:43.582
just in case we screwed
up really badly.

00:15:43.582 --> 00:15:45.290
MARK MANDEL: Or make
a copy or something.

00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:46.460
HOLDEN KARAU: Yeah, sure.

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:49.235
Copies are cheap, right?

00:15:49.235 --> 00:15:51.620
I don't actually know how
much we charge for anything.

00:15:51.620 --> 00:15:53.270
MARK MANDEL: Cloud
privilege, I understand that.

00:15:53.270 --> 00:15:55.728
MELANIE WARRICK: What's changed
to make both Beam and Spark

00:15:55.728 --> 00:15:59.090
put so much focus on
non-JVM languages?

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:00.590
HOLDEN KARAU: So I
think the biggest

00:16:00.590 --> 00:16:02.997
change is that deep learning
is very cool right now.

00:16:02.997 --> 00:16:05.330
And people are trying to raise
money in Silicon Valley--

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:06.140
I mean, we're not.

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:07.850
We are a publicly
traded company.

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:11.900
But a lot of the open source
companies are like, ooh,

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:13.530
I need to add some AI to my--

00:16:13.530 --> 00:16:15.946
MELANIE WARRICK: Spread some
AI on that and it looks good.

00:16:15.946 --> 00:16:18.134
HOLDEN KARAU: Yeah,
it looks a lot better.

00:16:18.134 --> 00:16:19.550
We're not just a
big data company,

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:21.400
we're a big data machine
learning company.

00:16:21.400 --> 00:16:23.150
But you know, big
data machine learning

00:16:23.150 --> 00:16:25.970
isn't as cool as big
data deep learning.

00:16:25.970 --> 00:16:28.680
There are some JVM
tools for deep learning.

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:31.640
But there's not quite the
same full range of options

00:16:31.640 --> 00:16:34.010
as there are in Python
or other languages.

00:16:34.010 --> 00:16:36.410
And a lot of people
really want to be

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:39.740
able to just copy and paste
the Python code and have it run

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:41.660
rather than translate
it into Java,

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:43.910
because it's a lot
less work for raising

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:45.380
your next round of funding.

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:48.950
And so really, it comes
down to that people in Java

00:16:48.950 --> 00:16:51.920
now are actually wanting
to use Python code.

00:16:51.920 --> 00:16:53.630
And this is really
exciting as someone

00:16:53.630 --> 00:16:56.370
whose job it is to make
Python and Java work together.

00:16:56.370 --> 00:16:58.370
That's my role to
a large degree.

00:16:58.370 --> 00:16:59.786
MELANIE WARRICK:
Those communities

00:16:59.786 --> 00:17:01.040
don't always get along, too.

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:02.498
HOLDEN KARAU: No,
historically they

00:17:02.498 --> 00:17:04.500
have very nice things
to say about each other.

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:05.750
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes, they do.

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:07.849
They very much respect,
wholeheartedly,

00:17:07.849 --> 00:17:09.855
each other's different
point of views on coding.

00:17:09.855 --> 00:17:11.150
But anyway, yes,
you were saying?

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:12.733
HOLDEN KARAU: Yeah,
it's super awkward

00:17:12.733 --> 00:17:14.630
when you're in between
those two communities.

00:17:14.630 --> 00:17:16.760
And you're just like, hm,
maybe let's stop making

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:19.020
fun of classes.

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:22.579
Or like, I know the drill is the
thing, but it's not that bad.

00:17:22.579 --> 00:17:23.640
Anyways, whatever.

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:25.646
MELANIE WARRICK: I actually
like both languages

00:17:25.646 --> 00:17:27.770
for different reasons and
don't like both languages

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:28.686
for different reasons.

00:17:28.686 --> 00:17:30.931
But yes, I can see where
the disconnect comes from.

00:17:30.931 --> 00:17:32.930
HOLDEN KARAU: But getting
back to the main point

00:17:32.930 --> 00:17:34.580
of your question,
people are now trying

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:36.347
to use Python code from Java.

00:17:36.347 --> 00:17:38.930
And it's really exciting because
the Python people have always

00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:41.820
been trying to use some
of the big data Java code.

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:43.670
And now that both sides
are working together

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:45.920
rather than just random
people like me hanging out

00:17:45.920 --> 00:17:47.930
in the middle, there's
a lot more interest.

00:17:47.930 --> 00:17:50.240
And so you see projects
like Apache Arrow which

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:52.400
are really exciting and
provide a common data

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:54.200
format between the
different languages

00:17:54.200 --> 00:17:56.002
so they can inter-operate
a lot better.

00:17:56.002 --> 00:17:57.710
And so this is, I
think, really exciting.

00:17:57.710 --> 00:17:59.510
MARK MANDEL: Is it just Python?

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:00.690
HOLDEN KARAU: No.

00:18:00.690 --> 00:18:02.390
Arrow is not just Python.

00:18:02.390 --> 00:18:05.630
It's Python, R, Java--

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:10.490
I think JavaScript, but
don't hold me to that one--

00:18:10.490 --> 00:18:11.240
and Go.

00:18:11.240 --> 00:18:12.740
People were talking about
it on the mailing list.

00:18:12.740 --> 00:18:14.990
And I'm sure there's more that
I'm just not keeping up with.

00:18:14.990 --> 00:18:16.698
I mostly care about
the Python community,

00:18:16.698 --> 00:18:18.060
but other things are good too.

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:18.980
MARK MANDEL: And
does that mean, then,

00:18:18.980 --> 00:18:21.570
you can use those languages
on Spark or Beam as well?

00:18:21.570 --> 00:18:23.930
Or is that just Python
and Java derivatives?

00:18:23.930 --> 00:18:25.610
HOLDEN KARAU:
Yeah, so it doesn't

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:28.130
mean that we can necessarily
use those languages together.

00:18:28.130 --> 00:18:31.346
In Spark, we can use both
Python and R. In Beam,

00:18:31.346 --> 00:18:32.720
there's actually
interesting work

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:35.690
being done to add support for
Go, which is actually something

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:38.330
that I demoed yesterday
at Flink Forward.

00:18:38.330 --> 00:18:42.560
Word count is the best demo
ever at a big data conference.

00:18:42.560 --> 00:18:44.570
We counted a lot of words--

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:46.212
genuine big data.

00:18:46.212 --> 00:18:47.920
So Beam doesn't actually
depend on Arrow.

00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:49.930
Because Beam has this
sort of Google lineage,

00:18:49.930 --> 00:18:52.790
it actually uses FlatBuffers
and other things like this which

00:18:52.790 --> 00:18:56.330
are very similar to Arrow
but are slightly different

00:18:56.330 --> 00:18:59.510
in pretty uninteresting ways
unless you really like C++

00:18:59.510 --> 00:19:00.740
code, which is fine too.

00:19:00.740 --> 00:19:02.750
It's more or less
unrelated, but they're

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:05.110
following similar approaches.

00:19:05.110 --> 00:19:07.340
And just because we
have a common format

00:19:07.340 --> 00:19:08.810
to get the data
between each other,

00:19:08.810 --> 00:19:10.250
there's this other
challenge where

00:19:10.250 --> 00:19:13.610
we have to figure out a
way to take our functions

00:19:13.610 --> 00:19:16.040
and serialize them and
send them across the wire,

00:19:16.040 --> 00:19:18.590
and deal with that in a
nice way for execution

00:19:18.590 --> 00:19:19.670
on another machine.

00:19:19.670 --> 00:19:22.610
And so in Python, that's
a moderate amount of work.

00:19:22.610 --> 00:19:26.250
And we use a weird custom
serializer to make it happen.

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:29.960
In Go, it's also a
non-trivial amount of work.

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:32.300
And so there's
other problems that

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:35.250
have to be solved to make
a language work here.

00:19:35.250 --> 00:19:38.349
But having a common data format
is one of the prerequisites.

00:19:38.349 --> 00:19:39.890
So it's exciting to
see that part get

00:19:39.890 --> 00:19:41.375
unlocked for more languages.

00:19:41.375 --> 00:19:42.125
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:19:42.125 --> 00:19:44.960
For someone who hasn't touched
Spark or Beam, what does

00:19:44.960 --> 00:19:46.950
the development cycle look like?

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:48.880
How does that whole process go?

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:50.930
Like if I have some
data and I want

00:19:50.930 --> 00:19:53.540
to process it to either
learn something about it

00:19:53.540 --> 00:19:55.532
or change it, what
does that look like?

00:19:55.532 --> 00:19:56.990
HOLDEN KARAU: So
they actually have

00:19:56.990 --> 00:20:01.480
slightly different processes
as common first paths.

00:20:01.480 --> 00:20:04.040
In Spark, it's really
common to get started

00:20:04.040 --> 00:20:06.980
by opening up a
notebook and just

00:20:06.980 --> 00:20:09.597
poking at your data for a
while to be like, I wonder--

00:20:09.597 --> 00:20:11.180
maybe it does-- no,
that doesn't work.

00:20:11.180 --> 00:20:13.370
OK, what about this?

00:20:13.370 --> 00:20:15.010
It's a very iterative nature.

00:20:15.010 --> 00:20:18.000
That tends to be more for
Python and Scala developers.

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:21.490
Java developers, you spin up
a new project, you make a JAR,

00:20:21.490 --> 00:20:23.860
you submit it to the cluster,
you take a look at it.

00:20:23.860 --> 00:20:25.351
And it's a little
more heavyweight

00:20:25.351 --> 00:20:27.100
when you're working
in Java, and it's also

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:29.599
a little more heavyweight when
my cost to test something out

00:20:29.599 --> 00:20:31.990
is compiling my project
and submitting the JAR.

00:20:31.990 --> 00:20:34.860
Even though like it may take
similar amounts of time,

00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:37.190
the mental overhead is
more than Shift+Enter.

00:20:37.190 --> 00:20:39.280
And so for Java
developers, it tends

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:41.950
to be more like you'll
print out your schemas,

00:20:41.950 --> 00:20:44.770
you'll look at it, you'll
make a quick test pipeline.

00:20:44.770 --> 00:20:47.170
And then you'll sample the
data and run it through

00:20:47.170 --> 00:20:48.380
to see what happens.

00:20:48.380 --> 00:20:51.370
And with Python and Scala
developers in the Spark land,

00:20:51.370 --> 00:20:53.924
you tend to use a notebook and
sort of poke at it that way.

00:20:53.924 --> 00:20:56.090
MARK MANDEL: So more like
an online REPL type thing.

00:20:56.090 --> 00:20:56.881
HOLDEN KARAU: Yeah.

00:20:56.881 --> 00:20:58.880
And then the Beam
online REPL thing--

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:01.300
it's not so happy just yet.

00:21:01.300 --> 00:21:04.300
It's more focused
around traditional jobs

00:21:04.300 --> 00:21:06.814
where you submit a
package to the cluster.

00:21:06.814 --> 00:21:08.230
MARK MANDEL: So
basically, I write

00:21:08.230 --> 00:21:10.525
some code in some language,
I submit it to the cluster,

00:21:10.525 --> 00:21:13.150
and it's the cluster's job that
splits that up and parallelizes

00:21:13.150 --> 00:21:13.884
that work?

00:21:13.884 --> 00:21:14.800
HOLDEN KARAU: Totally.

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:17.060
If you really want, you can do
things slightly differently.

00:21:17.060 --> 00:21:18.730
One of the things
which I do because I

00:21:18.730 --> 00:21:21.310
travel so much is I'll also--

00:21:21.310 --> 00:21:23.270
if I'm working with
non-sensitive data,

00:21:23.270 --> 00:21:26.480
I'll sample my data and save
it locally to my machine.

00:21:26.480 --> 00:21:28.990
And then both Beam and
Spark have this concept

00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:31.540
of a local mode runner
where you can just

00:21:31.540 --> 00:21:34.960
run your stuff against a local
copy of your data to just check

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:38.110
and make sure that it works so
your development cycle isn't

00:21:38.110 --> 00:21:38.650
super long.

00:21:38.650 --> 00:21:40.930
You're not always having
to submit something

00:21:40.930 --> 00:21:41.560
to the cluster.

00:21:41.560 --> 00:21:44.020
You can just try
stuff out locally

00:21:44.020 --> 00:21:47.200
before you run it on
your genuine "big data."

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:49.460
There's air quotes around
that, just to clarify.

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:50.730
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:21:50.730 --> 00:21:53.214
What type of machine
learning options are there?

00:21:53.214 --> 00:21:55.630
HOLDEN KARAU: In Spark, there's
a lot of different machine

00:21:55.630 --> 00:21:56.460
learning options.

00:21:56.460 --> 00:21:59.980
From a design point of view,
writing iterative algorithms

00:21:59.980 --> 00:22:02.870
in Spark is a lot easier than
writing iterative algorithms

00:22:02.870 --> 00:22:03.370
in Beam.

00:22:03.370 --> 00:22:05.230
So things like gradient
descent are just

00:22:05.230 --> 00:22:08.530
a little easier to write in
Spark just because of the way

00:22:08.530 --> 00:22:10.420
these two platforms
were developed.

00:22:10.420 --> 00:22:13.900
And so Spark has kind of a
head start and a lead there.

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:16.750
And you can do linear
regression, just

00:22:16.750 --> 00:22:18.610
classic, good old
fashioned stuff.

00:22:18.610 --> 00:22:20.080
You can make your
decision trees,

00:22:20.080 --> 00:22:22.390
you can make your
random forests,

00:22:22.390 --> 00:22:24.400
all of the classical
both regression

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:26.050
and classification algorithms.

00:22:26.050 --> 00:22:29.110
And then there's also this
collection of Spark packages

00:22:29.110 --> 00:22:30.580
which essentially comes from--

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:33.624
I mean, there's just not enough
ML reviewers inside of Spark.

00:22:33.624 --> 00:22:36.040
And when people show up with
new algorithms I'm just like,

00:22:36.040 --> 00:22:37.870
I could read your paper.

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:40.820
Or I could take a bath.

00:22:40.820 --> 00:22:41.541
And then--

00:22:41.541 --> 00:22:43.790
MELANIE WARRICK: And read
it while you're in the bath.

00:22:43.790 --> 00:22:45.970
HOLDEN KARAU: Yeah-- no.

00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:47.320
That's where email gets done.

00:22:49.084 --> 00:22:50.500
MELANIE WARRICK:
That's important.

00:22:50.500 --> 00:22:51.708
HOLDEN KARAU: Very important.

00:22:51.708 --> 00:22:53.500
But no, it's just
the amount of work

00:22:53.500 --> 00:22:55.750
to review an algorithm
for inclusion

00:22:55.750 --> 00:22:58.900
is really, really high
because you want to make sure

00:22:58.900 --> 00:22:59.884
it's correct.

00:22:59.884 --> 00:23:02.050
And you also want to make
sure that it's maintained.

00:23:02.050 --> 00:23:03.520
And nothing against
grad students.

00:23:03.520 --> 00:23:05.980
I love grad students
and their code.

00:23:05.980 --> 00:23:08.140
But pretty frequently,
a lot of people

00:23:08.140 --> 00:23:10.060
will show up with an
algorithm that they've

00:23:10.060 --> 00:23:13.000
implemented in Spark from
part of their grad project.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:14.990
And it's just like,
we'd love to take this,

00:23:14.990 --> 00:23:17.470
but we also know you're
going to graduate really soon

00:23:17.470 --> 00:23:20.890
and who's going to maintain this
when you go work in industry

00:23:20.890 --> 00:23:23.230
or go and become
a full professor?

00:23:23.230 --> 00:23:26.650
Maybe you'll get your
new, tiny grad students

00:23:26.650 --> 00:23:28.839
to look after this,
but I'm not sure.

00:23:28.839 --> 00:23:30.880
If you want to go and try
that grad student code,

00:23:30.880 --> 00:23:34.420
you can totally go to
Spark-packages.org and scroll

00:23:34.420 --> 00:23:37.600
through the list of grad
student machine learning models

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:40.330
and pick your favorite one,
and just hope they keep it up

00:23:40.330 --> 00:23:43.900
to date or your business process
changes before you need any bug

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:44.504
fixes.

00:23:44.504 --> 00:23:46.420
Or you can become a
machine learning developer

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:48.040
yourself, which is great.

00:23:48.040 --> 00:23:51.102
And if you make your business
process depend on it first,

00:23:51.102 --> 00:23:52.810
well, there's not a
lot your boss can do.

00:23:53.587 --> 00:23:56.170
MELANIE WARRICK: Whether you're
doing machine learning or not,

00:23:56.170 --> 00:23:59.284
what does it look like to
contribute to Beam and Spark?

00:23:59.284 --> 00:24:00.200
HOLDEN KARAU: Totally.

00:24:00.200 --> 00:24:02.230
So they're both
open source projects

00:24:02.230 --> 00:24:04.930
and they're both within
the Apache Foundation.

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:06.580
And this is cool
because it means

00:24:06.580 --> 00:24:09.934
that there's some common
elements of culture

00:24:09.934 --> 00:24:10.600
between the two.

00:24:10.600 --> 00:24:12.600
Obviously, the people
are different--

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:13.600
or sorry, not obviously.

00:24:13.600 --> 00:24:16.390
But the people are different
between the two to some degree.

00:24:16.390 --> 00:24:18.430
And so there are different
cultural practices

00:24:18.430 --> 00:24:19.090
that have developed.

00:24:19.090 --> 00:24:20.714
But there is sort of
a common framework

00:24:20.714 --> 00:24:22.480
for how you go about those.

00:24:22.480 --> 00:24:25.190
In both cases, you
start by subscribing

00:24:25.190 --> 00:24:26.440
to the developer mailing list.

00:24:26.440 --> 00:24:31.660
And it tends to be like
dev-subscribe@beam.apache.org

00:24:31.660 --> 00:24:33.974
or
dev-subscribe@spark.apache.org.

00:24:33.974 --> 00:24:35.890
And then you can subscribe
to the mailing list

00:24:35.890 --> 00:24:38.350
and you can start to see
what other developers are

00:24:38.350 --> 00:24:39.250
working on.

00:24:39.250 --> 00:24:42.460
And then you can also say, like,
hey, what's up, I'm new here.

00:24:42.460 --> 00:24:45.520
Does anyone want to
give me some guidance?

00:24:45.520 --> 00:24:47.020
If you do that on
the Spark project,

00:24:47.020 --> 00:24:49.214
there's a really good
chance you'll get ignored.

00:24:49.214 --> 00:24:51.380
If you do that in the Beam
project, it's more likely

00:24:51.380 --> 00:24:53.940
people will be like,
yay, new friends!

00:24:53.940 --> 00:24:57.730
And this is mostly just because
Beam is a younger open source

00:24:57.730 --> 00:24:58.780
project than Spark.

00:24:58.780 --> 00:25:03.220
And so it's easier to be like, I
don't have 200 other people I'm

00:25:03.220 --> 00:25:06.580
already working with, here's
a new person, let's do this.

00:25:06.580 --> 00:25:09.850
And in Spark, it's like
I have 200 open pull

00:25:09.850 --> 00:25:11.950
requests that I need to review.

00:25:11.950 --> 00:25:14.650
If you come with some code,
I'll help you, but not yet.

00:25:14.650 --> 00:25:17.590
And so that can be
a little different.

00:25:17.590 --> 00:25:20.380
In both cases, JIRA is
used to track the issues.

00:25:20.380 --> 00:25:22.610
You can go into JIRA, you
can look at open issues.

00:25:22.610 --> 00:25:24.526
You can look at things
which people have maybe

00:25:24.526 --> 00:25:25.924
tagged as starter issues.

00:25:25.924 --> 00:25:27.340
They're broken
down by components,

00:25:27.340 --> 00:25:30.370
so I myself, personally,
mostly go and look

00:25:30.370 --> 00:25:33.917
at either the Python or
ML issues in each project

00:25:33.917 --> 00:25:36.250
and see if there's anything
that people are encountering

00:25:36.250 --> 00:25:38.800
as either bugs or improvements
that I want to work on.

00:25:38.800 --> 00:25:41.260
And those are things
that I pick up and do.

00:25:41.260 --> 00:25:42.770
And then they're both on GitHub.

00:25:42.770 --> 00:25:44.790
They both accept pull requests.

00:25:44.790 --> 00:25:48.790
And you can sort
of go from there.

00:25:48.790 --> 00:25:51.340
They each have their own
contributing guide as well,

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:53.160
on their websites,
that you can look at.

00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:54.622
And they have style
guides as well.

00:25:54.622 --> 00:25:56.830
Because when you have open
source projects like this,

00:25:56.830 --> 00:25:59.155
you want a consistent
style even though we

00:25:59.155 --> 00:26:01.030
don't work at the same
company and don't have

00:26:01.030 --> 00:26:02.410
the same shared style guides.

00:26:02.410 --> 00:26:04.744
So each project
has its own style.

00:26:04.744 --> 00:26:06.160
Switching between
the two projects

00:26:06.160 --> 00:26:07.910
is great because the
styles are different.

00:26:07.910 --> 00:26:10.870
And that's just super easy
to keep straight in my head.

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:11.662
And I love my life.

00:26:11.662 --> 00:26:13.203
MELANIE WARRICK:
What are you excited

00:26:13.203 --> 00:26:14.800
about the work you're doing?

00:26:14.800 --> 00:26:16.510
HOLDEN KARAU: Well, there's
different kinds of excitement,

00:26:16.510 --> 00:26:16.670
right?

00:26:16.670 --> 00:26:18.378
There's excitement
about code and there's

00:26:18.378 --> 00:26:21.700
excitement about code
other people are writing.

00:26:21.700 --> 00:26:24.280
And then there's
excitement about people

00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:27.070
that I get to meet when I go
out into the open source world.

00:26:27.070 --> 00:26:29.710
So the excitement about code
that I'm writing is mostly--

00:26:29.710 --> 00:26:32.200
there's some stuff
around improving

00:26:32.200 --> 00:26:35.740
the speed at which Python and
Java are able to work together.

00:26:35.740 --> 00:26:37.240
And that is something
I'm personally

00:26:37.240 --> 00:26:39.040
really passionate about.

00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:41.440
And I'm excited to
make that more awesome.

00:26:41.440 --> 00:26:43.370
And I actually have a
talk at Spark Summit

00:26:43.370 --> 00:26:48.010
SF about how you can use that to
use TensorFlow inside of Spark.

00:26:48.010 --> 00:26:50.350
And you can use similar
techniques inside of Beam,

00:26:50.350 --> 00:26:52.720
but it's more complicated.

00:26:52.720 --> 00:26:55.620
And so that talk will
probably be a quarter later

00:26:55.620 --> 00:26:58.060
once they work out
some more bugs.

00:26:58.060 --> 00:27:00.490
So like, one thing
then the other thing.

00:27:00.490 --> 00:27:04.030
Code other people are writing
that I'm excited about--

00:27:04.030 --> 00:27:06.250
there's a lot of really
interesting exciting things

00:27:06.250 --> 00:27:08.050
in both projects which
are happening around

00:27:08.050 --> 00:27:10.305
sort of improving
the SQL experience.

00:27:10.305 --> 00:27:11.680
And I think this
is a thing which

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:15.670
is often overlooked, at least
among people like myself.

00:27:15.670 --> 00:27:17.890
MARK MANDEL: Is this like
a BigQuery experience type

00:27:17.890 --> 00:27:18.860
thing in Spark?

00:27:18.860 --> 00:27:20.260
Is that kind of how that works?

00:27:20.260 --> 00:27:22.240
HOLDEN KARAU: It's is
similar to BigQuery,

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:26.140
except it runs with the Spark
Engine so it's less managed.

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:28.244
And there's more of a
maintenance overhead.

00:27:28.244 --> 00:27:30.160
But the idea is that you
can write SQL queries

00:27:30.160 --> 00:27:32.080
against your
traditional big data.

00:27:32.080 --> 00:27:33.580
The nice thing
about that is if you

00:27:33.580 --> 00:27:35.788
work with someone from,
maybe, more of a data analyst

00:27:35.788 --> 00:27:38.606
background who might be more
comfortable working with SQL,

00:27:38.606 --> 00:27:40.480
they might produce some
really cool insights.

00:27:40.480 --> 00:27:43.180
And then it becomes your
job to productionize them.

00:27:43.180 --> 00:27:46.480
And then that can sometimes
mean rewriting SQL in the Java.

00:27:46.480 --> 00:27:49.440
And that's no units of fun.

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:52.330
But instead, because it has
an integrated SQL engine,

00:27:52.330 --> 00:27:56.380
you can just plop that SQL
statement in and then rewrite

00:27:56.380 --> 00:28:00.670
the critical path or
the expensive parts

00:28:00.670 --> 00:28:03.190
into your Java
code while keeping

00:28:03.190 --> 00:28:04.510
most of the existing SQL code.

00:28:04.510 --> 00:28:07.090
Or if the SQL code
runs fine, you

00:28:07.090 --> 00:28:09.550
can just plop it into your
JAR and consume it downstream

00:28:09.550 --> 00:28:11.160
in the rest of
your ETL pipeline.

00:28:11.160 --> 00:28:13.630
Anyway, so there's improvements
happening in both Beam

00:28:13.630 --> 00:28:16.990
and Spark around SQL, and
that's really exciting

00:28:16.990 --> 00:28:18.822
to see that happening.

00:28:18.822 --> 00:28:21.280
MELANIE WARRICK: Are there any
other improvements or future

00:28:21.280 --> 00:28:23.290
features that you're
looking forward to?

00:28:23.290 --> 00:28:24.370
HOLDEN KARAU: Yeah.

00:28:24.370 --> 00:28:26.570
In both cases, they're
open source projects.

00:28:26.570 --> 00:28:29.320
So there's features which
I think might happen.

00:28:29.320 --> 00:28:31.360
But it's really
difficult to say for sure

00:28:31.360 --> 00:28:33.400
what will happen because
it comes down to what

00:28:33.400 --> 00:28:35.110
the community ends up wanting.

00:28:35.110 --> 00:28:37.870
And even if, for example, I
were to show up with code,

00:28:37.870 --> 00:28:39.550
there's no guarantee
that that's going

00:28:39.550 --> 00:28:41.140
to be what the community wants.

00:28:41.140 --> 00:28:44.850
I can commit my stuff in Spark--
if we don't have a consensus,

00:28:44.850 --> 00:28:47.980
it's considered impolite
to make changes--

00:28:47.980 --> 00:28:49.450
more than impolite.

00:28:49.450 --> 00:28:52.250
But it's important that
we all work together.

00:28:52.250 --> 00:28:54.340
And there are things
which are far enough

00:28:54.340 --> 00:28:56.465
along that I'm pretty sure
they're going to happen.

00:28:56.465 --> 00:28:59.020
Like, the SQL improvements
are going to happen.

00:28:59.020 --> 00:29:01.505
And everything else
is a little more--

00:29:01.505 --> 00:29:04.320
eh, who knows if it'll happen?

00:29:04.320 --> 00:29:05.900
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:29:05.900 --> 00:29:09.004
You mentioned that you've got
a lot of talks that you do.

00:29:09.004 --> 00:29:10.420
Were there any
talks in particular

00:29:10.420 --> 00:29:12.280
that you wanted to
talk about that you're

00:29:12.280 --> 00:29:13.480
going to be doing soon?

00:29:13.480 --> 00:29:17.470
HOLDEN KARAU: Yeah, I have some
ones that I'm excited about.

00:29:17.470 --> 00:29:20.340
I'll be at PyCon
very soon as well.

00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:22.990
I'll actually go from
PyCon to Scala Days,

00:29:22.990 --> 00:29:26.440
which is very symbolic of
my life in a lot of ways.

00:29:26.440 --> 00:29:28.330
Both of those
talks are exciting.

00:29:28.330 --> 00:29:31.330
And I love both the Python and
Scala communities so I'm really

00:29:31.330 --> 00:29:33.550
looking forward to that in May.

00:29:33.550 --> 00:29:37.300
And I'll also be at Strata
London and then J on the Beach.

00:29:37.300 --> 00:29:39.040
I've never been to J
on the Beach before,

00:29:39.040 --> 00:29:40.706
but from what my
coworkers have told me,

00:29:40.706 --> 00:29:42.110
it's a really
