00:30:33.599 --> 00:30:35.140
HOLDEN KARAU: One
of the other things

00:30:35.140 --> 00:30:38.620
which I'm passionate about as
well is testing and validating

00:30:38.620 --> 00:30:40.130
these big data pipelines.

00:30:40.130 --> 00:30:41.380
I think it's really important.

00:30:41.380 --> 00:30:43.840
It's often overlooked because
it's a little harder to do

00:30:43.840 --> 00:30:45.460
than in our other projects.

00:30:45.460 --> 00:30:47.380
And a lot of humans,
when encountered

00:30:47.380 --> 00:30:50.740
with a problem slightly harder
which literally does not

00:30:50.740 --> 00:30:53.290
catch on fire, just
kind of walk around it.

00:30:53.290 --> 00:30:54.790
And while it doesn't
catch on fire,

00:30:54.790 --> 00:30:56.970
it does catch on fire later.

00:30:56.970 --> 00:31:00.070
In a little survey that I ran
with a few hundred respondents,

00:31:00.070 --> 00:31:03.070
15% of people said that their
big data pipelines cause

00:31:03.070 --> 00:31:05.570
serious production outages.

00:31:05.570 --> 00:31:07.300
And that's a lot.

00:31:07.300 --> 00:31:10.390
And I enjoy not having to
keep an up-to-date resume,

00:31:10.390 --> 00:31:12.490
and I assume other
people do too.

00:31:12.490 --> 00:31:14.620
I also don't want
the software projects

00:31:14.620 --> 00:31:17.140
that I work on to be
responsible for my death.

00:31:17.140 --> 00:31:18.700
So I think people
should definitely

00:31:18.700 --> 00:31:20.794
test their Spark and Beam code.

00:31:20.794 --> 00:31:22.960
In Spark, there is this
project called Spark Testing

00:31:22.960 --> 00:31:25.300
Base, which I work on,
which makes it easier

00:31:25.300 --> 00:31:26.440
to test your code.

00:31:26.440 --> 00:31:28.090
And in Beam, it's
actually really

00:31:28.090 --> 00:31:31.030
nice-- there's
built-in primitives

00:31:31.030 --> 00:31:32.560
for doing high-quality testing.

00:31:32.560 --> 00:31:35.470
There's things called P asserts
which make it really easy to do

00:31:35.470 --> 00:31:37.360
pipeline assertions in Beam.

00:31:37.360 --> 00:31:40.030
And I think they're really
cool and, perhaps, underused.

00:31:40.030 --> 00:31:42.850
And so I would always
encourage people-- please test

00:31:42.850 --> 00:31:44.404
and validate your pipelines.

00:31:44.404 --> 00:31:46.570
If you don't know how, I
have talks on this subject.

00:31:46.570 --> 00:31:47.350
