00:32:18.040 --> 00:32:18.835
Question of the week?

00:32:18.835 --> 00:32:19.510
MARK MANDEL:
Question of the week.

00:32:19.510 --> 00:32:21.634
MELANIE WARRICK: So I have
a continuous integration

00:32:21.634 --> 00:32:24.040
build process set up
with Container Builder.

00:32:24.040 --> 00:32:25.570
But it's all sequential.

00:32:25.570 --> 00:32:29.080
So to speed things up, how would
I basically process parts of it

00:32:29.080 --> 00:32:29.684
in parallel?

00:32:29.684 --> 00:32:31.600
MARK MANDEL: So I found
this out the other day

00:32:31.600 --> 00:32:33.555
and I got really,
really excited about it.

00:32:33.555 --> 00:32:34.930
I'll talk through,
basically, how

00:32:34.930 --> 00:32:36.970
I've got things set up so that
it properly gives some context.

00:32:36.970 --> 00:32:39.345
So I've got a continuous build
process running, strangely

00:32:39.345 --> 00:32:40.510
enough, for [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:40.510 --> 00:32:42.010
It has a bunch of build steps.

00:32:42.010 --> 00:32:44.461
I run tests, and then
I build some images.

00:32:44.461 --> 00:32:46.960
And then I put those images up
on Container Registry, pretty

00:32:46.960 --> 00:32:48.934
standard continuous
integration type things.

00:32:48.934 --> 00:32:50.350
But they're all
sequential, right?

00:32:50.350 --> 00:32:54.760
So I have to wait for my
tests to finish before I start

00:32:54.760 --> 00:32:56.382
building my container images.

00:32:56.382 --> 00:32:58.590
So I have to run my tests
and then build my binaries,

00:32:58.590 --> 00:33:00.220
then push my binaries
into the container image,

00:33:00.220 --> 00:33:01.210
and build the image.

00:33:01.210 --> 00:33:02.260
And it's all sequential.

00:33:02.260 --> 00:33:04.359
Now in theory, I
could run my tests

00:33:04.359 --> 00:33:06.400
at the same time I'm
building my binaries, right?

00:33:06.400 --> 00:33:07.870
There's really no
reason why I have

00:33:07.870 --> 00:33:09.078
to wait for one or the other.

00:33:09.078 --> 00:33:11.380
If either of them fail,
then that's it, we're done.

00:33:11.380 --> 00:33:13.796
And I didn't think you could
do this in Container Builder,

00:33:13.796 --> 00:33:14.920
but you can.

00:33:14.920 --> 00:33:16.870
There's basically a
configuration option

00:33:16.870 --> 00:33:17.944
called waitFor.

00:33:17.944 --> 00:33:19.360
We'll put a link
in the show notes

00:33:19.360 --> 00:33:20.721
to the exact documentation.

00:33:20.721 --> 00:33:22.720
But there's a configuration
option for each step

00:33:22.720 --> 00:33:24.050
that you can put in waitFor.

00:33:24.050 --> 00:33:27.129
You can put waitFor dash, which
just says start it right away,

00:33:27.129 --> 00:33:27.670
I don't care.

00:33:27.670 --> 00:33:29.336
I'm not going to wait
for anything else.

00:33:29.336 --> 00:33:31.330
But you can also
give IDs to steps.

00:33:31.330 --> 00:33:34.570
And then basically, you just
say, wait for this list of IDs.

00:33:34.570 --> 00:33:37.310
So you can do both splittings
or you can split out.

00:33:37.310 --> 00:33:40.176
So like first one starts,
and then three will go,

00:33:40.176 --> 00:33:41.800
and then you can do
joining back again.

00:33:41.800 --> 00:33:43.716
So you wait for all of
these steps to complete

00:33:43.716 --> 00:33:44.530
and it comes back.

00:33:44.530 --> 00:33:46.360
And so you can do parallel
processing and speed up your

00:33:46.360 --> 00:33:46.810
build.

00:33:46.810 --> 00:33:48.059
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

